Building my Home SOC Lab

    I decided to build my own home lab to get more hands-on practice with real-world infrastructure and defense scenarios.

    The Architecture: The entire setup runs on Proxmox, using both Virtual Machines and Containers (LXC/Docker). I used pfSense (Firewall) to segment the network into dedicated VLANs:
 – VLAN 10 (Red Team): My Kali Linux attack box.
 – VLAN 20 (Vulnerable Machines): A dedicated testing segment hosting DVWA, bWAPP, and other intentionally vulnerable containers/VMs used for attack simulation and log analysis.
 – VLAN 30 (Active Directory): A full Windows domain (Server & Win 11) with Sysmon configured for deep visibility.
 – LAN (Blue Team): The core of my monitoring, featuring a Wazuh (SIEM) 3-in-1 setup (Server, Dashboard, Indexer) that ingests logs from all agents and acts as a Syslog server for pfSense. I also use Nessus for vulnerability scanning.

    For remote access, I integrated Tailscale (VPN), allowing me to manage the lab from anywhere.

    AI-Powered Analysis: As a first step toward automation, I deployed Ollama on my local PC (since the server is currently RAM constrained). Using Wazuh’s Active Response, I wrote a Python script that forwards critical alerts to the local LLM. The AI analyzes the raw logs and sends a summary directly to my telegram bot. 

    What’s Next? Once I upgrade my server's RAM, I plan to move the LLM directly onto Proxmox. The next phase is implementing full automation using Shuffle (SOAR) for playbooks and TheHive for case management and ticketing. I’m also planning to give the AI access to the Kali to see how it handles evasion and attack execution.


Any recommendations on what I could add to take this infrastructure to the next level?

