Building my Home SOC Lab: Part 2

 Since my last post I made a few upgrades to the environment. I added a second Proxmox node and joined both servers into a cluster.

 Now alerts generated by Wazuh (SIEM) can automatically trigger playbooks inside Shuffle (SOAR). I built a couple of workflows to simulate how alerts move through a SOC pipeline.

 One workflow focuses on alert triage and notifications:
 – receive a Wazuh alert via webhook
 – create an alert in TheHive
 – extract and add observables
 – send a notification to my Telegram bot

 Another workflow simulates basic automated response:
 – receive a Wazuh alert
 – create and trigger a firewall rule on pfSense to block the suspicious IP
 – create a case in TheHive
 – enrich the IP using AbuseIPDB
 – add the indicator as an observable inside the case

 I also integrated Cortex and MISP directly with TheHive, which makes investigation much easier. From inside a case I can quickly run analyzers, enrich observables, generate reports, or pivot indicators into MISP to build a small internal threat intelligence dataset.

 And of course I couldn’t ignore the AI trend in cybersecurity. So I also built a small AI-powered penetration agent using MCP + Ollama that interacts with my vulnerable lab environment. The idea was to see how far a local LLM could go when allowed to probe an intentionally vulnerable application.

 Surprisingly, in about 10 minutes it managed to identify around three vulnerabilities on the lowest security level of DVWA.

